So, what can you do? You say, well, I don't have a lot of friends that are men. I don't have a lot of these relationships. So what can you do?
Number one, you can reach out to other men. You can reach out to other men. One of the interesting statements that was part of the men's movement of the 1980s was this idea that American men are in waiting. American men are in waiting. Kind of this passivity among American men that they're sitting back waiting for somebody. They're waiting for their dad to step into their life. They're waiting for their wife to show them love. They're waiting for their kids to finally listen to them and respect them. They're waiting for their employer to finally value their contribution. What I would suggest is is that waiting is the opposite impulse of true masculinity. True masculinity has the impulse of initiating, taking charge, moving in, being the one who leads rather than the one who waits. And if you don't have men in your life, the answer to that is what are you going to do about it? You initiate.
See, a feminine response to feeling like, well, there's no other women in my life, would be to say, what's wrong with me? Doesn't anybody like me? Why doesn't the church provide a service where people are like paired up with other people so that I can kind of operate out of somebody reaching out to me? That's because the feminine mode of operation is more relational in nature. The male mode of operation is more initiating. And so if you're not initiating, the men in your life think you don't want any friends. Because if you did, being a man, you would do what? You'd step out there and do something about it. Okay? You'd step out there and do something about it.
Secondly, what you can do is challenge a man at a deeper level. So approach other men and challenge them at a deeper level. In other words, verbalize. A great example of this in 1 Samuel chapter 20, Jonathan and David, they actually put words to their relationship. They made a covenant with each other and swore their loyalty to each other that they would have their backs. And they stood together through thick and thin. And both of them lived a hugely noble life. And a lot of that was driven out of this masculine relationship. And so to call a guy up and say, listen, I'm looking for some people that I can build a strong friendship with. I would like to sit down with you and get to know you and start talking about the areas in our life that we can grow together. Actually open your mouth and verbalize what you're looking for.
The third one is pick someone you can enjoy life with. Pick someone that you can enjoy life with. In other words, think about what you like to do, what your interests are and hobbies. Look for other guys that like the same thing. I mean, let's be practical. You're going to spend more time with people that kind of like the same things that you like. And so, if you're into monster trucks, you're probably not going to become tight with a guy who's into classic art. Nothing wrong with either of those. They probably won't put you together. But I would say though, don't discount a person at face value. Take the time to get to know them. If you want men in your life, initiate, go after them. Start building those relationships.
